"""Original non-clinical trauma-informed service and coordination teaching."""
def course(c,m):
 return c("SWP-052","Trauma Informed Support and Service Coordination","trauma-informed-support-and-service-coordination","Trauma Informed\nSupport and Service\nCoordination",
 "Review a fictional service encounter and coordination pathway for choice, predictable communication, restricted information, qualified referral and accountable organisational support.",[
m("Trauma-informed service encounters","Identify service practices that preserve agency without diagnosing trauma or prescribing treatment.",[
"A trauma-informed approach considers how an encounter may affect a person who has experienced distress or violence, while recognising that the worker may not know their history. It does not require every person to disclose trauma or accept a diagnostic label. Explain the role, the purpose of a question and what choices are available, and make the next step as predictable as possible. The provider-perspective synthesis identifies agency, relationships and organisational conditions as relevant concerns. Its qualitative findings are a basis for reflective questions, not proof that a particular communication technique produces clinical recovery. [R22, S1]",
"Ask permission before changes in the interaction, including involving another person or requesting sensitive information. Offer a pause, an accessible format or an alternative way to complete a necessary task where feasible. Respect cultural and individual communication preferences without presuming that a group label determines what is appropriate. Do not interpret silence, distress, a changed account or difficulty completing a form as evidence of dishonesty. The inter-agency standards support survivor-centred and non-discriminatory services, while the actual adjustment must fit the person's preferences, safety and the service's authority and resources. [S10, S1]",
"Keep supportive conduct separate from trauma therapy. A non-clinical learner can practise a respectful introduction, clear explanation, non-blaming acknowledgement and bounded referral information. They should not diagnose post-traumatic stress, ask a person to relive an experience, prescribe treatment or promise that a simple exercise will resolve symptoms. WHO's 2025 health-worker training overview emphasises professional communication and further skills development in a clinical workforce context. This academy course uses that map as a comparator and does not reproduce WHO exercises or confer a clinical qualification. [S13, S11, S1]",
"Use only the supplied fictional adult cases for practice. Participation should not depend on disclosing personal experiences, and a learner may pause or choose an alternative fictional exercise. Feedback should assess reasoning about choice, scope and the environment rather than the emotional intensity of a performance. Independent observation and supervised practice are still needed before a professional skill can be considered established. A service should also examine its own procedures and power, because a reassuring tone cannot compensate for coercive rules, inaccessible contact or an unqualified referral pathway. These are questions for accountable organisational review as well as individual reflection. [R22, S10, S13]"],
"Fictional case: an adult is distressed by an unexplained change of staff during an appointment. The worker explains the proposed change and choices, asks about communication needs and offers a feasible pause. The worker does not request a trauma history or attempt therapy to complete the appointment.",
"Rewrite the fictional encounter with a role explanation, a permission check, a feasible choice, an access adjustment and a professional boundary. Identify one organisational issue that supportive wording alone cannot resolve.",
"A trauma-informed encounter preserves agency and predictability without requiring disclosure or treatment. Appropriate wording matters alongside actual service rules, resources and qualified support.",["R22","S1","S10","S13","S11"],[
("Trauma-informed conduct does not require disclosure.","A worker wants every applicant to recount trauma before receiving ordinary assistance. What fits this course?","Provide respectful predictable support without requiring a trauma history.",["Make disclosure compulsory to show engagement.","Assign a diagnosis from a short form.","Use personal histories as classroom performance material."],"Respectful service can be offered without collecting a trauma narrative."),
("Predictability can support agency.","An appointment will change staff unexpectedly. What should the worker explain?","The proposed change, relevant choices and feasible arrangements before proceeding.",["Nothing, because explanations reduce efficiency.","A guarantee that the change cannot be distressing.","That accepting every change is a condition of dignity."],"Clear explanation allows the person to understand and influence the next step."),
("Distress does not establish dishonesty.","An adult has difficulty completing a form while distressed. What is appropriate?","Explore a feasible supportive adjustment without treating distress as proof of dishonesty.",["Assume the account is false.","Demand a detailed retelling to test credibility.","Punish a request to pause."],"A service difficulty is not a basis for an unsupported credibility judgement."),
("Non-clinical support differs from trauma therapy.","A learner proposes asking someone to relive an experience as treatment. What boundary applies?","Trauma therapy requires the relevant qualified role and appropriate clinical process.",["The course title alone authorises treatment.","Any distressing role-play is automatically therapeutic.","A journal citation replaces clinical competence."],"The course teaches service support and referral rather than trauma processing."),
("Supportive wording cannot replace adequate systems.","A worker speaks kindly but the service still imposes coercive access rules. What needs attention?","The organisational rules and their effect on choice as well as the interaction.",["Only the tone, because rules cannot affect experience.","A claim that kindness proves recovery.","More personal disclosures from applicants."],"Trauma-informed review concerns systems and power, not wording alone."),
]),
m("Coordinated support and qualified referral","Build a bounded service-coordination pathway that avoids repeated stories and unsupported promises.",[
"Coordination links relevant roles around an agreed priority without making the person responsible for resolving every gap between services. Start with what the person wants help with and which role can respond, then check whether the proposed support is actually available and accessible. A list of organisations is not a functioning pathway. The inter-agency standards distinguish psychosocial support, case management and referral arrangements, while qualified specialist services may involve several of these functions. This introductory exercise identifies the boundaries and dependencies; it does not train a learner to provide every component of care. [S10, S11]",
"Consent and purpose continue across organisational boundaries. Explain what a receiving role needs, why information would be shared and what the person can choose, subject to verified applicable limits. Avoid repeatedly requesting a full narrative because different teams use different forms. Use necessary information through the authorised process and do not create a shared public record or broad email chain of sensitive details. Specialist case-management guidance provides a professional information framework; it is not permission for informal data exchange or for a worker to decide another service's legal obligations without qualified advice. [S11, S1]",
"A clinical, protection, legal or safeguarding need should connect to the relevant qualified role through the applicable arrangements. Prompt professional assessment may be needed for an urgent concern, and a non-clinical coordination task should not delay that response while compiling a complete plan. Do not diagnose, prescribe, collect forensic evidence or guarantee a legal outcome. WHO's health-worker curriculum overview identifies a professional learning context that is different from this academy introduction. When children, adolescents or supported decision-making are involved, do not apply an adult fictional case as a universal protocol; seek the appropriate specialist process. [S13, S11, S1]",
"Assign responsibility for the next agreed step and its unresolved conditions. Confirm whether the receiving role has accepted the request, what communication is safe and whether the person wants follow-up. An inaccessible contact or an unavailable service should prompt an honest discussion of feasible options, not a repeated referral cycle or a promise that cannot be kept. Provider-perspective research identifies fragmentation and resource constraints as concerns, but does not demonstrate that an additional coordination meeting improves survivor outcomes. Review actual access and the person's priorities through an approved process rather than count messages as recovery. [R22, S10, S11]"],
"Fictional case: a person has been asked to repeat the same account at several services but still cannot reach the intended support. The learner proposes a qualified coordinating role, purpose-limited information sharing and a confirmed next contact, with an unresolved capacity gap recorded honestly.",
"Draw a simple textual pathway for the fictional case: chosen priority, qualified role, permission and information scope, capacity status, next action owner and safe follow-up condition. Identify a step that should not wait for a complete plan if qualified urgent attention is needed.",
"The pathway should reduce unneeded retelling, verify actual roles and preserve permission and communication boundaries. A referral count or meeting is not proof of accessible care or improved wellbeing.",["S10","S11","S1","S13","R22"],[
("Coordination needs functioning roles rather than a list.","A pathway names many agencies but none has accepted a responsibility. What is missing?","Confirmed appropriate roles, capacity and an agreed next step.",["Only more agency logos.","A larger contact count as proof of access.","A claim that naming an agency creates a commitment."],"A useful pathway depends on actual responsibility and access."),
("Repeated retelling is not automatically necessary for coordination.","Several services request full narratives for an access enquiry. What should be reviewed?","Whether necessary purpose-limited information can support the agreed pathway without repeated disclosure.",["A rule that every form requires every detail.","Publication of one shared unrestricted narrative.","Permission to bypass consent because coordination is difficult."],"Information should match purpose within appropriate safeguards."),
("Urgent professional support should not wait for administrative completeness.","A potentially urgent clinical concern arises while a plan is unfinished. What should a non-clinical worker facilitate?","Prompt qualified assessment through the applicable arrangements.",["Completion of every non-urgent form before access.","A diagnosis made from the coordination map.","A medication plan produced by the learner."],"Administrative planning should not delay appropriate urgent care."),
("Receiving capacity should remain explicit.","A specialist service cannot confirm availability. What should the pathway show?","The unresolved capacity condition and an appropriate owner for considering feasible options.",["Guaranteed admission despite the uncertainty.","A completed-care outcome.","A hidden gap to keep the report positive."],"Transparent limits support a realistic next decision."),
("Coordination outputs are distinct from outcomes.","A programme counts case meetings as proof of recovery. What should the interpretation acknowledge?","Meetings are activities; access and wellbeing outcomes need separate appropriate evidence.",["More meetings always mean better recovery.","No survivor perspective is relevant.","A meeting count is a clinical measure."],"Activity volume does not establish a personal outcome."),
]),
m("Team conditions and accountable learning","Identify organisational and supervisory responses to service pressure without substituting self-care for adequate systems.",[
"Consistent support depends on organisational conditions as well as individual goodwill. Appropriate staffing, supervision, access arrangements, clear responsibilities and realistic service availability affect what a team can deliver. The qualitative provider synthesis identifies both interaction-level and system-level concerns across several high-income Western contexts. It does not prove that a local service can achieve those conditions without resources or that individual resilience can compensate for an unsafe workload. In the fictional review, distinguish what the worker can adjust from decisions requiring a manager, a specialist supervisor or another accountable role. [R22, S10]",
"Workers may need confidential professional support when difficult work affects them, while protecting survivors' information and maintaining role boundaries. Use authorised supervision and appropriate staff-support arrangements rather than compulsory public retelling or sharing identifiable case material with friends or classmates. The case-management guidance includes supervision and staff care within service quality. This course does not prescribe treatment for workers or label ordinary distress as a diagnosis. A personal coping activity cannot replace management action on a harmful workload, missing training, harassment or an insecure information system. Identify the relevant responsibility instead of assigning every organisational problem to self-care. [S11, S1]",
"Gather feedback through an appropriate safe process that does not require disclosure as the price of assistance. Ask whether people understood the next step, could express a preference and reached the intended support, while noting who could not contribute. Counts of training attendance, contacts or closed cases do not establish quality or recovery on their own. The programme standards support monitoring and accountable learning, but a local review needs proportionate evidence and permissions. Staff accounts can identify constraints; they should sit alongside appropriately gathered service-user perspectives rather than substitute for them. [S10, R22, S1]",
"Create an improvement note with the observed process concern, evidence and limits, responsible decision-maker, feasible action, resource dependency and review point. Provide an appropriate response to concerns or corrections and retain the approved decision history without unnecessary identifiers. If a service cannot meet a condition, record the gap and next responsibility instead of relabelling it as readiness. WHO's training overview also points to further skills practice; neither a draft lesson, a positive feedback form nor a course MCQ pass establishes observed specialist competence. Academic, specialist and supervised practice review remain separate from this production build. [S13, S11, S1]"],
"Fictional case: a team faces an excessive workload and unavailable supervisory cover. A manager offers a self-care leaflet but does not address the service conditions. The learner identifies managerial responsibilities, an appropriate confidential support route and a bounded improvement review without using identifiable case examples.",
"Write an improvement note with process concern, evidence limit, worker and management responsibilities, confidential support route, resource condition and review point. Explain why a self-care leaflet or attendance count cannot establish service readiness.",
"A useful response assigns organisational responsibility and supports workers without unnecessary disclosure. It combines appropriate supervision, realistic resources and service feedback rather than claiming that personal coping or training attendance proves competence.",["R22","S10","S11","S1","S13"],[
("Organisational conditions affect service quality.","A worker lacks supervision and has an unsafe workload. What should the service review address?","Staffing, supervision and management responsibilities alongside individual support.",["Only the worker's personal motivation.","A claim that goodwill supplies missing capacity.","A new success label without changing any condition."],"Service systems shape what an individual can safely and competently deliver."),
("Self-care does not replace management action.","A manager responds to excessive workload only with a self-care leaflet. What remains unresolved?","The workload and organisational responsibilities requiring an appropriate decision.",["Nothing, because a leaflet guarantees readiness.","The need to blame workers for every system problem.","A duty for workers to disclose case details publicly."],"Personal coping support is distinct from correcting service conditions."),
("Worker support should protect survivor information.","A staff member seeks support after difficult work. What is appropriate?","Use authorised confidential support and supervision without unnecessary identifying case details.",["Post identifiable narratives to a social group.","Require compulsory public retelling of every case.","Upload personal case files to an unapproved tool."],"Support needs do not justify broad disclosure."),
("Attendance alone does not establish observed competence.","A team completes training attendance forms but no skills observation. What conclusion is justified?","Attendance occurred; relevant skills still require appropriate observation and review.",["Every specialist skill is proven.","The service is automatically accredited.","All survivor outcomes have improved."],"Completion counts and demonstrated professional capability are different evidence."),
("Improvement notes need accountable follow-through.","Feedback identifies a repeated access barrier. What should an improvement note include?","A responsible role, feasible action, dependency and review point with evidence limits.",["Only a positive slogan.","Deletion of the concern to protect the programme image.","A universal recovery claim from one comment."],"An accountable response connects evidence to an authorised action and later review."),
]),
])
